gearing/data/*.json text eol=lf

# The tracker's own data files, for the same reason gearing/data already had it — and
# because the mismatch actively blocks work. Every writer here emits LF
# (JSON.stringify(...) + "\n"), while core.autocrlf=true checks the files out as CRLF, so
# git reported a phantom modification the moment any script touched one: `git status`
# showed M while `git diff` was empty. That is not cosmetic — the local-run skill refuses
# to bootstrap on a dirty tree, so a refresh could be blocked by a file whose content had
# not changed at all (2026-08-08). Pinning eol=lf makes the working tree agree with both
# the index and the writers.
data/*.json         text eol=lf
data/history/*.json text eol=lf

# Built HTML must stay LF in the WORKING TREE, not just in the blob. Both artifacts carry
# a Content-Security-Policy whose script-src is a sha256 of their inline script, and the
# build hashes LF because the HTML parser normalizes newlines before the browser hashes.
# With core.autocrlf=true (the default on Windows) a fresh checkout would rewrite these to
# CRLF, the hash would no longer match, and the browser would silently refuse to run the
# app — a blank page from `git clone` alone, with nothing in the diff to explain it. That
# also protects gearing/README.md's promise that the single file can just be opened.
dist/*.html                 text eol=lf
gearing/wow-s2-gearing.html text eol=lf

# The pre-staged flip-day test patch must apply byte-exact with `git apply` on the day —
# an autocrlf rewrite at checkout would make its hunks whitespace-mismatch the LF test
# files it targets. Same LF pin for the season-archive records: data/*.json does not
# reach subdirectories (which is why data/history/ is listed explicitly above).
docs/s2-flip-test-patch.diff text eol=lf
data/season-archive/*.json   text eol=lf
# data/forecasts/ for the same reason as season-archive/, and it was the one immutable lane
# left unpinned (audit 2026-08-14). `data/*.json` does not reach subdirectories. These hold
# the frozen forecast the post-launch report card grades — an artifact whose whole value is
# being byte-stable, re-derivable and hash-checkable months later, which a CRLF rewrite at
# checkout would quietly undermine.
data/forecasts/*.json        text eol=lf
